  ],
  [
    "1966",
    "Around the middle of the year the pearloid dots give way to large rectangular pearloid block inlays on the now-bound board. That pairing is the standard CBS-era Jazzmaster neck and it runs to the end of this page's range.",
    "Block inlays on a bound board mean mid-1966 or later, with no exceptions worth arguing about.",
  ],
  [
    "1967",
    "Around the middle of the year the headstock shape is modified to the outline used on post-1965 Stratocasters. It is a subtle change rather than the Stratocaster's obvious enlargement, and it shows best in a direct comparison. Late in the year the black CBS logo arrives and the Jazzmaster model name changes to a bolder block-letter style. Polyester finish comes in around 1967 into 1968.",
    "A black logo with a blockier model name beside it.",
  ],
  [
    "1968 to 1971",
    "The specification holds. Bound board, block inlays, witch hat knobs, F tuners, black logo, polyester finish.",
    "Nothing new to separate these years. The neck stamp and the pot codes do the work.",
  ],
];

// The checks that place a Jazzmaster in an era without tools. These give a WINDOW.
const fastRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["The pickguard", "Gold anodized aluminum to mid-1959, celluloid tortoiseshell to late 1964, plastic tortoiseshell after. White instead of tortoiseshell on custom colors", "The single most useful thing on the guitar, and the screw count comes with it: nine on the gold guard, thirteen from mid-1959 on."],
  ["The inlays", "Clay dots to late 1964, pearloid dots to mid-1966, block inlays after", "Three eras rather than two. Blocks also mean a bound board, so those two always travel together."],
  ["The knobs", "Strat-style dome knobs to mid-1965, witch hat knobs after", "The cleanest CBS line on the instrument, and readable from a photograph."],
  ["The fingerboard", "Slab rosewood to spring 1962, veneer after", "Look at the rosewood depth at the nut. The Jazzmaster made this change before the Stratocaster did."],
  ["The logo", "Spaghetti to mid-1964, transition to 1967, black CBS after", "The first two are both gold, so go by the weight of the script and not the color. Thin and delicate, then the same gold drawn much heavier, then black. The headstock outline barely changes, so the decal does the work."],
];

// Jazzmaster neck-heel model code. Sourced for the STAMPED era only.
const codeRows: [string, string][] = [
  ["4", "Jazzmaster, in the rubber-stamped heel codes used from mid-1962 (for example 4JAN63B)"],
];

// Serial plate eras. Named, NOT charted. Charts live on the hub.
const plateRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["Plain chrome four-bolt plate", "1958 to late 1962", "Four or five digits, no letter. Fender pre-stamped plates in batches and pulled them off the stack in no order, so these overlap heavily year to year."],
  ["L-prefix plate", "Late 1962 to late 1965", "An L in front of five digits. The last of the pre-CBS plates."],
  ["Big-F plate", "About the third quarter of 1965 to 1976", "Six digits stamped above a large stylized F. The CBS plate."],
];

const tocItems: [string, string][] = [
  ["#start", "Start Here"],
  ["#fast", "The Five Fastest Checks"],
  ["#years", "Jazzmaster Year By Year"],
  ["#guard", "The Pickguards, All Of Them"],
  ["#neck", "The Neck, Board And Inlays"],
  ["#electronics", "Pickups And The Dual Circuit"],
  ["#bridge", "Bridge, Tremolo And Hardware"],
