];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What Year Is My Jazzmaster?",
    a: "Work it in three passes and expect each to give you a window rather than an answer. Start with the pickguard and the inlays, because between them they cut the whole run into slices from across the room. Then read the knobs, the tuners, the board, and the serial number together, treating the serial as one vote among several. Then open it up for the parts Fender actually dated: the neck heel stamp and the body date, backed up by the pot codes. Those last two are the only things that will give you a month.",
  },
  {
    q: "What Is A Gold Guard Jazzmaster?",
    a: "One of the first ones. Fender fitted a gold anodized aluminum pickguard from the 1958 launch to about the middle of 1959, roughly six to nine months of production, before switching to celluloid tortoiseshell. Gold guard examples are the rarest and most valuable Jazzmasters there are. The aluminum was not decoration either: it shielded the wide, hum-sensitive pickups, working alongside the reverse-wound pickup pair. Because they carry such a premium, replacement gold guards do get fitted to later guitars, so confirm any gold guard against the neck heel date, the body date, and the pot codes before you believe it.",
  },
  {
    q: "How Many Screws Should My Jazzmaster Pickguard Have?",
    a: "Nine if it is a gold anodized guard from 1958 to mid-1959, and thirteen from then to the end of the run. Fender added the extra fixings when it moved to celluloid, because celluloid warps and shrinks and needed holding flat. It is one of the quickest checks on the guitar, and a thirteen-screw body wearing a nine-screw gold guard is telling you something.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why Does My Custom Color Jazzmaster Have A White Pickguard Instead Of Tortoiseshell?",
    a: "Because that is correct. Once the tortoiseshell era began in mid-1959, sunburst guitars got tortoiseshell and custom color guitars got white. It is not a later replacement and it is not somebody's taste, it is how they left the factory. On the celluloid ones that white ages toward mint green, the same way a nitrate Stratocaster guard does.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is My Jazzmaster Pre-CBS?",
    a: "Pre-CBS means built before CBS completed the purchase in January 1965, but the guitars did not change on that date. On a Jazzmaster the useful markers cluster slightly later: witch hat knobs from mid-1965, binding and F tuners from late 1965, block inlays from mid-1966. A guitar with none of those and clay dots is comfortably pre-CBS. One with pearloid dots, Kluson tuners, and dome knobs is sitting right in the transition, which is exactly where the calendar stops helping and the features take over.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Jazzmaster Sounds Weak And The Pickups Sit Low. What's Wrong?",
    a: "Almost certainly foam rot, and it is not damage. Fender spring-loaded each pickup upward on a block of open-cell foam rather than screws and springs, and after fifty or sixty years that foam collapses, hardens, or crumbles away entirely. The pickups then drop into their routes, which costs output and dulls the tone. Replacing it with modern open-cell foam cut to size is standard maintenance rather than a modification, and evidence that the original foam was there is a point in the guitar's favor.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do Jazzmaster Pickups Have Staggered Pole Pieces?",
    a: "No. Fender never staggered them at the factory on this model, so the poles sit level or very slightly curved. That is worth knowing because it differs from a Stratocaster, where staggered poles are correct from 1954 and flat poles on a 1960s guitar are wrong. You also cannot check it from above on a Jazzmaster, because the cover has no pole holes, so the pickup has to come out.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I Take My Jazzmaster Apart To Date It?",
    a: "Only if you have done it before. Everything in the first two passes reads with the guitar assembled, which is deliberate. Old celluloid is brittle and an original tortoiseshell guard cracks at the screw ears if you rush it, and a gold anodized guard scratches if you so much as slip with a screwdriver. If the outside checks leave you stuck between two years, send me photos before you reach for a tool.",
  },
];

